Neural Networks in PyTorch & Transformer Foundations

Date: September 5, 2026
Platform / Environment: AWS AI & ML Scholars / Udacity Nanodegree — PyTorch

What I Did

Completed a section of my Nanodegree covering neural networks in PyTorch, data processing for language models, attention mechanisms, training and validation, and LLM architectures.

I worked through:

* Building multi-layer neural networks using PyTorch tensors and nn.Module.
* Using Sigmoid and ReLU activations and understanding logits and Softmax outputs.
* Creating a custom PyTorch Dataset for tokenised text sequences.
* Using DataLoader and RandomSampler for batched training data.
* Learning the roles of Query, Key and Value in self-attention.
* Reviewing Transformer components including multi-head attention, layer normalisation, residual connections and feedforward layers.
* Creating training and validation pipelines.
* Implementing a PyTorch training loop with forward propagation, cross-entropy loss, backpropagation and optimizer updates.
* Comparing the development and scaling of GPT-1, GPT-2 and GPT-3.
* Reviewing Decoder-Only, Encoder-Decoder and Encoder-Only Transformer architectures.

What I Learned

One of the biggest lessons was seeing how the different parts of a neural-network training process connect.

A simplified view is:

Input → Model → Prediction → Loss → Backpropagation → Weight Update → Repeat

I also developed a better understanding of how text can be converted into token IDs and organised into input/target sequences for language-model training.

The attention section helped me understand that Transformers use Query, Key and Value representations to determine which parts of the input should receive more attention.

Key Takeaways

* PyTorch provides building blocks for constructing and training neural networks.
* Dataset and DataLoader help organise and feed training data to a model.
* Validation loss provides a way to evaluate how the model performs on data separate from its training data.
* Self-attention is a core mechanism behind Transformer models.
* Transformer architectures can be designed differently depending on the task.
* GPT-style models use a Decoder-Only architecture designed for autoregressive text generation.

Practical Relevance

This gave me a stronger foundation for understanding how modern language models are structured and trained, rather than treating LLMs as a black box.

It also gave me practical experience with PyTorch code and the basic components that appear in machine-learning training pipelines.

Next Step

Continue applying these concepts in the Nanodegree projects and practise building and training small PyTorch models so that I become more comfortable with the code rather than only understanding the theory.
